ZERO WEB JAVASCRIPT PROFILE - EXPERIMENTAL

Implementation

javascript.zero.part is original Zero source. It uses no Chromium/V8 code,
external runtime, native code generation or host API. This is a small,
deliberately bounded interoperability profile, not a complete ECMAScript
implementation and not general Chromium compatibility.

Supported statements

let name = expression;
const name = expression;
var name = expression;
name = expression;
document.getElementById('id').textContent = expression;

Whitespace is allowed between tokens. Statements require semicolons between
them; the last semicolon is optional. Empty statements and // line comments
or /* block comments */ are accepted. Unterminated comments fail.

Bindings are document-wide, retain values between classic script elements in
tree order, and are discarded at the beginning of the next document request.
There are at most64 bindings; names are1..31 ASCII letters/digits/_/$ and cannot
begin with a digit or be a reserved word. Duplicate declarations, including
var declarations, are rejected by this restricted profile. A const cannot be
assigned again. Binding values are copied, not shared references.

Expressions

Operands are nonnegative decimal integers0..9007199254740991, quoted strings,
or existing binding names. Only the + binary operator is accepted, evaluated
left to right. Two integers add if the exact result fits the safe integer bound.
If either operand is a string, decimal conversion and string concatenation
follow the JavaScript behavior for these two supported types.

Strings may use single or double quotes and the escapes backslash, single
quote, double quote, n, r and t. Raw controls, numeric escapes, Unicode escapes,
template literals, interpolations, numbers with leading zeroes, fractions,
exponents, negative numbers, other operators, parentheses and function calls
outside the documented DOM assignment form are unsupported. String values
are at most511 bytes. The DOM parser has already validated source UTF-8;
the worker copies valid UTF-8 bytes without introducing an encoding converter.

DOM effect

getElementById accepts a literal string of1..63 bytes and chooses the first
element with the exact, case-sensitive ID. Missing elements fail rather than
silently dropping a write. Assignment replaces the element's owned text field,
sets replacement flag1, and converts a numeric value to decimal text. The
renderer must suppress former descendants of such an element. This is a
restricted tree mutation, not the browser-standard live DOM API.

Execution boundary

Only kind4 nodes with an empty type, text/javascript or application/javascript
execute. Type comparisons are ASCII-insensitive and trim surrounding spaces.
Other types are inert. module is inert and sets warning4. Python and JSON
types are not executed. External scripts are not fetched by this worker.
There is no file, network, process, eval, import, worker or timer API. Loops and
functions are unsupported. All scanning/evaluation loops have bounds derived
from input size, identifier count or string size.

This memory-only worker assumes the caller's valid4MiB arena and valid DOM.
Descriptor node count must be<=512, document input<=65536 bytes, and every
executed script's source span must lie inside the declared input span without
overflow. Node strings and destination fields follow declarations.zero.part.
The input bytes are not modified. This is not an OS process sandbox or a
general pointer validator.

Worker handshake

Fork context is web+1408, stack web+1245184 with81920 bytes, parent context is
web+1024. After including the worker, caller must switch to it once to perform
the initial handshake. Every subsequent switch processes one document.
Private scratch is web+740000..839999. Only @web and @v0..@v31 are used.

Failure contract

An incoming nonzero document error is preserved and no work is done.
New failures set error65 for malformed/unsupported syntax or missing bindings,
28 for bounded capacity exhaustion,70 for descriptor/source span violation,
and warning bit4. A script may have already changed the private DOM before a
later error. Caller MUST discard the entire failed document and must never
publish any partial rendering or claim the script completed.

Verification on25 November2026

javascript-check.zero composes declarations, javascript-check-head, worker,
and javascript-check-tail, in that order. It executes64 semantic/boundary
cases and256 deterministic malformed ASCII probes. Each case verifies the
input checksum and guards surrounding worker scratch and complete arena.
Cases exercise bindings across scripts and reset across requests, const,
duplicates, missing IDs, numeric bounds and overflow, + coercion ordering,
escapes, comment syntax, types and modules,64/65 binding limit,511/512 byte
strings, concatenation overflow, malformed spans, unsupported eval/fetch and
loops, and whole-page failure after an earlier DOM mutation.

Both nativeLinux x64 and the Windows x64 executable underWine passed all320
cases with exit0. The tests were compiled with the project's own0.14 Zero
compiler. Windows hardware, full browser integration and arbitrary websites
are not established by these worker tests.
